<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: #ffffff; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Healing Plants of Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Clarify misunderstandings in group discussions. Communication: Interpret cultural differences in communication.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can clarify misunderstandings in group discussions by pointing to visual aids (e.g., points to a picture of sabila (aloe vera) and says, "This is for burns.").
            Can interpret cultural differences in communication by explaining remedies and using culturally relevant expressions or gestures (e.g., says, "This is used to heal." while pointing to a leaf and using the Panamanian expression dale to indicate agreement or encouragement).
            Can explain simple plant remedies for peers, using visuals (e.g., points to a leaf and says, "This helps skin.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of healing plants commonly found in Panama (e.g., sábila, hierba de limón, menta, jengibre). Students work in pairs to name the plants they recognize and share one known use for each. The teacher prompts with questions like, "What is this plant?" and "What is it good for?" to activate prior knowledge related to the theme "Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns." and the scenario "The Healing Plants of Panama."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to clarify a misunderstanding about a plant's use, demonstrating effective mediation strategies. The teacher shows a picture of mint leaves and says, "Some people think mint is used to treat burns, but that's not right." Then, the teacher points to the picture of mint leaves and clarifies, "Mint leaves are *used to soothe* headaches. For burns, *you should use* sábila." The teacher stresses the first syllable of 'treatment' and 'therapeutic' while modeling correct pronunciation. The teacher also demonstrates using a culturally relevant gesture like a slight head nod for agreement.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If my friend says 'Ginger is for cleaning your house,' what should I clarify? Is ginger used for cleaning or for stomachaches?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I explain something and my friend looks confused, what can I do to help them understand? (e.g., point to a picture, use simpler words, make a gesture like 'dale')?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents "Healing Plant Remedy Cards." Each card includes a picture of a plant (e.g., sábila, guanábana, hierba de limón), its name, and a short description of its traditional uses. Some descriptions might contain a common misconception to be clarified, or simply present factual information that needs to be explained. For example: "Sábila (Aloe Vera): *Used to treat* burns and soothe skin. *You should apply* the gel directly to the affected area." "Guanábana (Soursop): *It is used to treat* infections and boost immunity." "Hierba de Limón (Lemongrass): *The leaves are good for* colds and fevers. *It is used to prepare* a tea." This input focuses on the infinitive of purpose, 'should/shouldn't', and present simple for facts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated "Healing Plant Remedy Cards" displayed on the board or projected, featuring plants like aloe vera, soursop, lemongrass, ginger, mint leaves.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Present and exchange simple health-related advice and remedies in discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What is the main idea of these cards?" "Are all these plants for the same problems?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students answer T/F questions based on the cards. E.g., "Soursop is used to treat infections. (T/F)" "You should boil sábila for burns. (T/F)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match plant names to their correct uses as described on the cards. E.g., "Aloe Vera" to "treats burns."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Infinitive of purpose: used to + infinitive verb; Should/shouldn't for giving advice; Present simple for facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> plant, leaf, root, remedy, ginger, infections, treatment, herb, application, soursop, aloe vera, lemongrass, mint leaves; treat, heal, soothe, apply, grow, boil, prepare; useful, medicinal, natural, therapeutic; for, with; traditionally, safely.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Practice pronunciation of compound nouns (e.g., "herbal garden," stressing the first syllable), application of syllable division rules (e.g., "treatment," "therapeutic").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Giving advice on usage: "You should use sábila (aloe vera) for burns."; Stating purpose: "Guanábana (soursop) is used to treat infections."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> Students complete sentences using "used to" or "should/shouldn't" with a given verb. E.g., "Ginger is ____ ____ (treat) stomachache." (used to treat) "You ____ ____ (not boil) aloe vera." (should not boil). This activity includes compound nouns and words like 'treatment' and 'therapeutic' to practice pronunciation and syllable division.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Clarification Dialogues:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence stems for mini-dialogues where one student states a misconception, and the other clarifies. Example: "A: I think lemongrass is for infections. B: No, lemongrass is *used to prepare* tea for colds. *You should try* soursop for infections." Students are encouraged to point to the "Healing Plant Remedy Cards" for visual support and use simple gestures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Explain and Clarify:</b> Students are given new "remedy cards" (or shuffled existing ones) in pairs. Student A explains a remedy to Student B using visuals (the card). Student B intentionally misunderstands or asks a clarifying question ("So, aloe vera is for colds, right?"). Student A must then clarify using the target language, visuals, and perhaps gestures (e.g., shaking head and pointing, saying "No, *sábila is used to heal* skin. *It's useful for* burns, like this!" while pointing to a burn picture).</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task: Healing Plant Remedies Mini-Exposition</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4) to present their "Healing Plant Remedies" project. Each group prepares a simple presentation (e.g., a poster, large cards) listing 2-3 common healing plants and their uses, incorporating advice structures ("You should try this for colds."). They must include visuals (drawings, cut-outs). During the presentation, one student explains a plant, while others act as "experts" ready to clarify. The audience is encouraged to ask questions, some of which might be intentionally misleading, requiring the presenters to mediate and clarify misunderstandings using the target grammar and vocabulary, and visual aids. They are prompted to use gestures and expressions like "dale" to show agreement or encouragement when explaining cultural uses. For instance, a student might point to a picture of aloe vera and say, "*This is used to heal* burns. *It's a natural remedy*, dale!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group presentation / Mini-Exposition.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion, with a strong emphasis on mediation strategies for explaining and clarifying.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills, plus peer assessment using a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Healing Plant Remedies Mini-Exposition," demonstrating their ability to explain plant remedies clearly, clarify misunderstandings in group discussions, and interpret cultural differences in communication using relevant expressions or gestures. Each student explains at least one plant's use and clarifies at least one misconception from the audience.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of compound nouns like 'herbal garden' and multi-syllable words like 'treatment,' 'therapeutic').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and accurate use of target vocabulary (plant, leaf, root, remedy, heal, apply, infections, etc.).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of grammar: "used to + infinitive," "should/shouldn't," and present simple for facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining plant remedies for peers, using visuals (e.g., points to a leaf and says, "This helps skin.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to clarify misunderstandings in group discussions by pointing to visual aids or rephrasing (e.g., "This is for burns, not colds.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Interpretation of cultural communication by using relevant expressions or gestures (e.g., "This is used to heal," while pointing to a leaf and using the Panamanian expression "dale").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one plant remedy you explained very clearly today in your presentation?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did you help a peer or audience member understand when they were confused about a plant's use?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I learn today about mediating (explaining and clarifying) information about healing plants to my classmates?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing I can do better next time to make my explanations even clearer or to help someone understand a different perspective?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting mediation skills (e.g., the use of visual aids, the clarification dialogues, the mini-exposition format)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in clarifying misunderstandings or using the target grammar (e.g., consistent use of "used to," 'should/shouldn't')? What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the theme "Sáblia Is Helpful for Burns." and the unit "The Healing Plants of Panama." The next lesson will introduce a new theme and scenario, building on the communicative and mediation skills practiced here by applying them to new contexts and information exchange.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>